their paths and added with `git add --sparse`; re-record with the recorder if the hash differs.)

## Interface the tool layer calls (crate mixdog-office)
`mixdog_office::inspect`:
- `InspectContext<'a, L: WriteLane, H: ComHost> { snapshot: SnapshotContext<'a, InLane<L>, H>, soffice: &Soffice, validator: &ooxml_validator::Environment,
  reviews: &ReviewSnapshots, abort: Option<Arc<AtomicBool>> }`. The lane proof is the one the snapshot context holds (the schema validator wants an
  `InLane` of a write lane). `validator` is `Environment::from_process()` in the product. `abort` is raised by the caller when the session's
  `active_signal` is cancelled (the blocking engines, the schema validator and LibreOffice, read a flag; the PDF readers also look at the session's
  signal themselves); the preview engine's `CancelFlag` is the pattern. The future is not `Send`: drive it on the lane thread.
- `issues(&cx, &handle, &args) -> Result<Value, String>`, `validate(...)`, `get_office_element(...)`, and
  `query_office_document(&cx, &handle, &args, cwd: &Path, note: &dyn WriteNote)` (every file a PDF query writes is reported to `note` first; the
  caller passes the call's ledger). All async only for the Office host's answers.
- `review_snapshot(&cx, &handle, &args)` (`reviewSnapshot`: the whole document once per snapshot version; qa and the render review call it too) and
  `ReviewSnapshots` (the per-session cache, at most 16 sessions; the tool layer owns one and calls `forget(session id)` when a session is closed).
- `csv_cell` (RFC 4180 quoting).
`mixdog_office::inline_audit`: `inline_office_audit(&cx, &handle, operations: &[Value], exclude: &[String]) -> Option<Value>` (`None` for a format with
no package; `{status:"unavailable"...}` when the inspection fails), `summarize_office_audit`, `touched_locations`, `record_inline_audit_round(&mut Session,
audit)` (the round counter lives in `Session.caches.inline_audit` as `{"rounds": n}`), `INLINE_AUDIT_TOP`, `INLINE_AUDIT_MAX_ROUNDS`.

## Not exercised by the twin
- The `compatibility: true` reopen calls `Soffice::validate_reopen` on the process's driver (`Soffice::process()`); the twin does not exercise it (it
  needs LibreOffice).
